module generic_call.pos_1

// TWO instances of ONE body: `dbl[int]` and `dbl[f64]` become two C functions,
// each typed under its own substitution (E2-b2, donor: Go stenciling).
//
// The body does arithmetic ON the parameter, and that is the point of the
// fixture: a body ignoring `x` would pass while nothing whatever had been
// substituted, which is the weak-fixture class (registry #465). Here the
// emission has to substitute `T` in the signature, in the mangled name and in
// the operand types -- `+` on int is the checked helper, on f64 it is the raw C
// operator, so a substitution that silently answered "int" would print a
// different number.
fn dbl[T](x T) -> T => x + x

fn main() {
    println(dbl[int](21))
    println(dbl[f64](1.5))
}
